"# A small style guide

Made by Alfred Borden.

## Who this is for, and what it is not

This is for someone new to Motoi Scheme who wants their programs to look and
feel right. It is a set of **general guidelines** — the habits that experienced
Schemers reach for without thinking. It is not a rulebook. Nothing here says
*you must write it this way*. Your program will run exactly the same whether or
not you follow a single line of it. These are suggestions from people who have
read a lot of code and noticed what makes it easy to live with.

Read it once. Keep what helps. Come back when a program starts to feel messy.

## The guidelines

### Indent with two spaces. No tabs.

When a form spans more than one line, indent the inside by two spaces. Do it
again for each level you nest. Two spaces is enough to see the shape and small
enough that deep code still fits on the screen.

```
(define (greet name)
  (if (string? name)
      (string-append \"hi, \" name)
      \"hi, stranger\"))
```

Use actual spaces, not tab characters. A tab looks different in every editor;
two spaces look the same everywhere, so everyone — and every model — sees the
same shape.

### Let the closing parens gather.

Scheme ends forms with a run of closing parens. Do not put each one on its own
line, and do not add spaces before them. Let them pile up at the end of the last
line that needs them. The indentation already tells the reader where each form
begins; the trailing parens just close the door behind you.

```
(define (sum-of-squares xs)
  (fold + 0 (map (lambda (x) (* x x)) xs)))
```

### Name things so the name tells you what they are.

- Words in a name are joined with hyphens: `next-frame`, `player-score`.
- A question — something that answers true or false — ends in a question mark:
  `empty?`, `even?`, `game-over?`.
- Something that changes state in place ends in an exclamation mark:
  `set!`, `clear!`, `spawn!`.

These are old Scheme habits. When you follow them, a reader knows what a name
does before they ever look up its definition.

### Keep each definition small, one idea at a time.

If a `define` grows past a screen, that is usually two ideas wearing one coat.
Pull the inside piece out, give it a name, and let the outer one call it. Small
named pieces are easier to read, easier to test, and easier to reuse.

### Write comments about *why*, not *what*.

The code already says what it does. A good comment says the thing the code
cannot: why this way and not the obvious other way, a limit you are working
around, a rule that would surprise the next reader. If a comment just restates
the line below it, the line below it did not need help.

## Why bother — this is really about compression

Here is the honest reason to keep a tidy, regular style, and it is the same
reason whether the reader is a person or a model.

A regular shape **compresses**. When every program indents the same way, closes
the same way, and names things the same way, there is far less to hold in your
head. You stop spending attention on *how is this one laid out?* and spend it on
*what is this one doing?* The surprising, meaningful parts of a program stand out
because everything around them is predictable. That is compression: the ordinary
parts take almost no room in your mind, leaving room for the parts that matter.

A language model reads the same way, only more so. A model learns by predicting
what comes next. When code follows a steady, regular shape, the ordinary parts
*are* predictable — the model spends almost nothing on them and can put its
effort into the real logic. Messy, one-off formatting is noise: it makes every
line a little surprising, and surprise is expensive. Tidy code is low-surprise
code, and low-surprise code is easier for a model to read, to learn from, and to
write back to you correctly.

So the two payoffs are the same payoff. What you do to make a program easy for a
person to hold in their head is the very same thing that makes it easy for the
model to predict. Compression for humans is compression for the model. That is
why these small habits are worth keeping — not because a rule says so, but
because a regular shape gives everyone reading it, carbon or silicon, more room
to think.

## The short version

Two-space indents, no tabs. Let the parens gather. Name questions with `?` and
changers with `!`. Keep definitions small. Comment the *why*. And if you forget
all of it, the one idea to keep is this: **a regular shape is a kindness** — to
the next person, to the model, and to yourself six months from now.")
